# COMM(1)

## NAME

comm - select or reject lines common to two sorted files

## SYNOPSIS

| **comm** [ **-** [ **123** ] ] file1 file2

## DESCRIPTION

*Comm* reads *file1* and *file2,* which should be ordered in the collating sequence described
below, and produces a three column output: lines only in *file1;* lines only in *file2;* and
lines in both files. The filename `-` means the standard input.

Flags 1, 2, or 3 suppress printing of the corresponding column. Thus **comm -12** prints only
the lines common to the two files; **comm -23** prints only lines in the first file but not in
the second; **comm -123** is a no-op.

## THE COLLATING SEQUENCE

*Note:* lines are compared a byte at a time, and a byte is an unsigned value from 0 to 255 on
this machine where it was a signed one on the PDP-11. So a character above U+007F - which is
more than one byte in this system's UTF-8 text - sorts *after* every ASCII character, not
before it as it did in v7. That is the order sort(1) and the shell's filename generation
already use, so

> sort file1 >a; sort file2 >b; comm a b

means what it looks like it means for a file holding **привет**.

## BUGS

A line longer than 255 bytes is cut, and what is left of it is treated as the line that
follows.

## SEE ALSO

cmp(1), diff(1), uniq(1), sort(1)
